---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base>
	<section class="hero">
		<h1>Lewis Lakerink</h1>
		<p class="tagline">
			Software engineer at Swinburne University of Technology. After hours: reverse
			engineering, embedded systems, and home automation — prying vendor lock-in out of
			my own house, one bus at a time.
		</p>
		<div class="status-line">
			<span><span class="dot"></span>home infra: operational</span>
			<span><span class="dot"></span>aircon bridge: live since 2026-08-12</span>
			<span><span class="dot"></span>tlsr8266 mesh: rust in production</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="about">
		<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>about</h2>
		<p>
			I build and break things across the software/hardware boundary — Rust on
			microcontrollers and Pi-class boards, Android internals when a vendor gets in my
			way, and a general fondness for reverse engineering the systems I own. Most of it
			lives on my <a href="https://github.com/retsimx">GitHub</a>; the longer write-ups
			live here.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section id="projects">
		<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>projects</h2>

		<div class="card">
			<h3>Project CB — MyAir Control Bridge</h3>
			<div class="card-meta">reverse engineering · rust · rs-485 · home hvac</div>
			<p>
				Replacing the vendor control path of a ducted Advantage Air / MyAir system:
				repackaged vendor apps, a Kotlin reimplementation of the service, a Rust
				register-mailbox daemon, and a Pi Zero W in the ceiling. Five-part write-up.
			</p>
			<div class="card-links">
				<a href="/projects/myair-bridge">read the series →</a>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/cb-daemon">cb-daemon</a>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/aaservice">aaservice</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<div class="card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<h3>Reverse Engineering the TLSR8266: From Vendor Blobs to LLVM and Rust</h3>
			<div class="card-meta">reverse engineering · rust · llvm · tc32 · ble mesh · rp2040</div>
			<p>
				Replacing the proprietary, closed-source C SDK on TYBT1/TLSR8266 smart lights with pure
				Rust: an overclocked RP2040 SWS programmer, Ghidra decompilation of proprietary link-layer
				blobs, an LLVM backend fork for Telink TC32, and an async lighting runtime with 32,767
				discrete easing levels. Five-part write-up.
			</p>
			<div class="card-links">
				<a href="/projects/tlsr8266-firmware">read the series →</a>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/tlsr8266_mesh">tlsr8266_mesh</a>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/llvm-project/tree/tc32">llvm-project (tc32)</a>
			</div>
		</div>

		<h3>other things I've built</h3>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/garagedoorcontroller">garagedoorcontroller</a>
				· <a href="https://github.com/retsimx/garagelight">garagelight</a> ·
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/garagebeam">garagebeam</a> — a Pico W / Rust
				garage stack: door control, lighting, and a beam sensor
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/skylights">skylights</a> — ESP32 control of
				Velux skylights over MQTT
			</li>
			<li>
				<a href="https://github.com/retsimx/crosbridge-ai">crosbridge-ai</a> — an
				OpenAI-compatible bridge to the ChromeOS Prompt API / NPU from Crostini
			</li>
		</ul>
	</section>
</Base>
